{0 URLs}

{!Workero.Request.url} returns a {!Workero.URL.t} for an incoming request.
Use {!Workero.URL.path} to match a decoded path and {!Workero.URL.query} to
read a decoded query value:

{@ocaml[
let handler req _env _ctx =
  let open Workero in
  let url = Request.url req in
  match URL.path url with
  | "/hello" ->
      let name = Option.value (URL.query url "name") ~default:"world" in
      Response.text (Printf.sprintf "hello, %s\n" name)
  | _ -> Response.text ~status:`Not_Found "not found\n"

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

{!Workero.URL} wraps the JavaScript [URL] and follows WHATWG parsing rules.
{!Workero.URL.of_string} accepts absolute [http] and [https] URLs. It returns
[None] for other schemes or invalid input. {!Workero.Request.url} raises
[Invalid_argument] if the incoming URL is not an [http] or [https] URL.

{1 Raw and decoded paths}

{!Workero.URL.path} decodes percent escapes: ["/a%2Fb"] becomes ["/a/b"].
{!Workero.URL.pathname} and {!Workero.Request.path} keep the percent-encoded
path. Use the raw path if encoded slashes must remain distinct from path
separators. A malformed escape such as [%zz] leaves the whole [URL.path]
undecoded. A [+] in a path stays [+].

Query values follow URL search-parameter rules: [+] becomes a space.
{!Workero.URL.query} returns the first matching value;
{!Workero.URL.query_all} returns all matching values in order. A key without
[=] has the value [""]. To keep the original query encoding, use
{!Workero.Request.query_string} or {!Workero.URL.search}.

See {!Workero.URL} for URL parts, all query pairs, and normalized URL output.
See {{!page-requests}Requests and responses} for methods and headers.
